## 01 · The Problem

Codemind v1 solves single-repo code intelligence well. v2.0 addresses three failure modes that emerge at scale — all caused by the same root: **agents and engineers operate on a single-repo view of the world.**

### Failure Mode 1 — Agents Don’t Know What They’ll Break

An agent changes a shared library function. It passes CI. Three sprints later a downstream service fails in production. The cross-repo dependency was never mapped. The agent had no way to know.

**Documented rate: ~40% cross-repo agent drift in teams with 30%+ AI-assisted commits.**

### Failure Mode 2 — No Safe Merge Order Across Repos

Multi-repo features require dependency-first merge sequencing. Agents and humans both get this wrong. Mabl’s own engineering team documented that their internal coordination system fails beyond 3 repos. There is no product that computes and enforces the correct order.

**Mabl public post, April 2026: fails beyond 3 repos without a dedicated coordination layer.**

### Failure Mode 3 — Interface Contracts Break Silently

A function signature changes. An OpenAPI spec drifts. A protobuf field is renamed. The consuming repo doesn’t know until runtime. No tool diffs contracts across repo boundaries at PR time.

**GitHub data: AI-generated PRs produce 10.83 issues/PR vs 6.45 for human-written code. Unresolved semantic conflicts are the primary driver.**

### Root Cause

No existing tool maintains a live, queryable graph of how repos depend on each other, what interfaces connect them, and what breaks if those interfaces change. Codemind already has the hardest part — the index. **v2.0 turns that index into a cross-repo coordination runtime.**

